Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the pandemic turned our lives upside  down altering the way we work and play  even where we live  [Music]  hello hello  welcome to my humble about  while many were looking for more space  william mansur decided to downsize  trading his shared town home for an  eight meter houseboat i love it i love  sailing and i love the community that's  built around it  though he admits the living quarters are  cramped  mansur is a computer programmer last  year his company closed its offices  forcing employees to work remotely  permanently the move celebrated by some  left mansur in a tight spot  it's easy to get claustrophobic and so i  needed to find a space where i could  work  he found a solution just a short bike  ride away at this downtown toronto  office building  mansur now pays 300 a month for a desk  at this co-working space  it's worth it he says for the extra  legroom and the community he missed  being around other people  and he's not alone  [Music]  definitely there's a lot of loneliness  but there's also  you know loneliness in a crowded house  for a lot of people too just prior to  the pandemic meredith garrettson  launched this vancouver co-working space  for women i kind of just fell in love  with the idea of having a space with  other women that were building their  businesses and we could kind of share in  that experience  then during the pandemic garrison's  personal life took a turn her marriage  fell apart her mother was diagnosed with  dementia  and her co-working colleagues became a  lifeline  i already had this network that i was  able to  turn into and i didn't tell everybody  right away  but eventually they became my safety net  and so  in a way that i thought that i was  building something for somebody else it  turns out i really benefited quite a bit  i think as people we naturally crave  social interaction i think that's the  reason we're seeing renewed interest in  these co-working spaces  [Music]  andrew au is a work culture consultant  we're going to keep this  entertaining as well as educational  his business is helping other businesses  adopt better ways of working  the pandemic has changed business i'd  say that the past decade  the focus has been on customer  experience you know predicting customer  needs personalizing the customer  experience but i think what's happening  now is the pendulum has swung to  employee experience  what do employees need  at the start of this year about a third  of canadians worked mostly at home  compared with just four percent a few  years ago  [Music]  for many including al that meant less  time commuting and more spent with his  family  but working from home surrounded by  children and chores has its own  challenges  working from home can be chaotic  and so that's why we wanted to give  employees a space to kind of get away  from that  all right jeff welcome to the intercept  cottage  so oww's company intercept purchased  this lakefront condo in cottage country  about an hour's drive north of toronto  it's available to his employees for free  as a space to work  you can have your laptop you could be  sitting at the beach club  and then you kind of look out and this  is your scene  al points to a global survey that  recently found three quarters of workers  prefer a flexible hybrid model a  combination of working from home and the  office or somewhere else that's what  they're demanding so as much as we are  in a technology race  we are in a talent race and if we want  to win that race  we need to offer flexibility and choice  to to employees  flexibility can also boost productivity  research has found remote workers  typically work longer are more  productive and less likely to quit  but what about those who can't work from  home or require close collaboration with  teammates a lot of us have spent the  pandemic sitting at our desks  interacting with our colleagues over  zoom but some emerging technology is now  altering that reality  [Music]  for 40 years ontario aerospace company  mda has trained astronauts to operate  the canadarm the canadian made robotic  arms used on space shuttles and the  international space station but when the  pandemic hit the team was grounded  unable to conduct its training  that is until jessa marie zabala and her  team had an idea that seemed out of this  world i  spend my meetings in space  through virtual reality  prior to the pandemic the engineer had  experimented with augmented reality  headsets they project holograms across a  physical space  so these tools are also  on board the international space station  wow  today trainers in brampton work  alongside flight controllers in montreal  and astronauts in houston all  interacting with the same space station  hi susan  hi  her colleagues appear as avatars  basically floating heads that mimic  their actual movements  you get that experience of like oh you  hear them right next to you on like this  side instead of like being on zoom and  people talking over one another and  cutting out this felt like a real  conversation mixed reality tech has been  adopted by a range of industries from  this paper mill in quebec to canada's  oldest candy company in new brunswick  when a piece of equipment at the ganong  factory breaks down the manufacturer  used to send someone often overseas but  in the pandemic they started connecting  and assisting virtually  in virtual reality it feels like you're  looking under the hood of a car but  under the hood of our hardware and  looking at all of like our mechanisms  while they're moving  is is pretty incredible  businesses are also deploying new  technology to limit infection in the  workplace moving beyond plexiglas to  airborne pathogen detection systems  employees will return to the office if  they feel safe  they have to feel safe and comfortable  so much has changed in such a short time  but what happens when the pandemic  finally ends is there any going back to  the way it was before i think work has  changed permanently  i think that some form of hybrid will be  the norm and hybrid will mean different  things to different employers  if employers don't adapt owl warrants  employees will vote with their feet  or in mansur's case they're sails  [Music]  we're really going  [Music]  when winter comes mansour may give up  the boat but he says he'll never return  to a traditional office job  i enjoy the freedom of being able to  work from different places new horizons  have opened so i think we will chase  those  [Music]

